  "statement": "The proposed National AI Injury Compensation Program (NAIICP) fundamentally misapprehends the nature of AI risk, offering post-hoc compensation for harms that are, at their true scale, uncompensable and irreversible, failing to address the civilizational stakes involved. Our institutional guardrails were built over centuries to protect society from systemic breakdown. Certain technological failures transcend individual remedy. The Chernobyl disaster, for instance, inflicted unrecoverable ecological damage and long-term health crises across a continent. No compensation fund could ever truly redress these costs. Similarly, documented emergent behaviors and specification gaming in advanced AI models reveal a potential for systemic destabilization. This scale of harm extends far beyond individual injury. An excise tax and limited liability are dangerously inadequate for such threats. This program benefits developers by capping their liability. The public bears the uncompensable costs of true systemic collapse.\n\nGranting limited liability immunity to AI developers without demonstrably verifiable, pre-deployment safety guarantees for all potential harm vectors is a profound dereliction of institutional stewardship. It undermines continuous human control. The fundamental epistemic opacity of neural architectures means formal verification and mechanistic interpretability remain unsolved research challenges. Consequently, 'tiered federal safety standards' are currently unenforceable fictions. Congress mandated rigorous pre-deployment certification and continuous oversight for the aviation industry. Unrecoverable failure modes, like those seen in the Boeing 737 MAX crashes that killed 346 people, demand absolute certainty before deployment. Delegating AI safety verification to the entities that stand to gain from speed, without independent, verifiable proof, repeats a fatal historical error.\n\nThis program risks creating a false sense of security. It diverts critical resources and attention from the fundamental, preventative safety research and stringent regulation required to address the root causes of AI misalignment and catastrophic risk. Historical precedent, such as the Superfund program, demonstrates compensation schemes often fail to address root causes. They can create a moral hazard. The existence of a fund might reduce the urgency for genuine prevention. The rapid pace of AI capability scaling far outstrips progress in fundamental safety research. Establishing a national compensation program before solving core issues of AI alignment and control is akin to building a vast national hospital network for a novel pandemic. It does so without investing in vaccine development or public health infrastructure. This strategic misdirection prioritizes reactive payment over proactive defense. It leaves society vulnerable to future, preventable harms.",
